#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079239} :activity/id #uuid "f525d904-1978-5a65-97fe-fc43e27af14e" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048436 :topic/id #uuid "9f06bd65-df18-5d83-abc2-a397390391ec" :topic/math-academy-id 666} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079239 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079635 :tutorial/content "Consider the equation \n\n$$\nx^2 +  y^2 - 2x - 4y = 4.\n$$\n\nBelieve it or not, this equation actually represents a circle! Its graph is given below:\n\n![](images/5d/5dfea5042d6c84f40a2790a01ee6aafb64433f7422ce88b589cf670d93771e2c.png)\n\n   \n\nClearly, this is a circle of radius $3$ centered at $(1,2).$ But how can we determine the center and radius from the given circle equation?\n\nLet's start with the equation\n\n$$\nx^2 +  y^2 - 2x - 4y = 4.\n$$\n\nBy grouping the $x$ and $y$ terms and completing the squares, we can write the equation in the standard form\n\n$$\n(x-a)^2 + (y-b)^2 = r^2.\n$$\n\nWe carry out the process with the following steps:\n\n**Step 1. **Group the $x$ and $y$ terms.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2x-4y & =4 \\\\ ({x}^{2}-2x)+({y}^{2}-4y) & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2.** In each group, add the square of half the linear coefficient to both sides of the equation. \n\n- Here, the $x$ coefficient is $-2,$ so we add $\\left( \\dfrac{-2}{2} \\right)^2 = (-1)^2 = 1$ to both sides.\n\n- Likewise, the $y$ coefficient is $-4,$ so we add $\\left( \\dfrac{-4}{2} \\right)^2 = (-2)^2 = 4$ to both sides.\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}^{2}-2x+1)+({y}^{2}-4y+4) & =4+1+4 \\\\ ({x}^{2}-2x+1)+({y}^{2}-4y+4) & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Finally, we factor each group into a perfect square, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-1)^{2}+(y-2)^{2} & =9 \\\\ (x-1)^{2}+(y-2)^{2} & ={3}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can see that the equation represents a circle centered at $(1,2)$ with radius $3.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "a8743599-a677-56de-8b27-1b1b04f19643" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2440 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "5a84eb88-0914-5665-a28e-3f5d962dbbb0" :step/math-academy-id 10942 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079240}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079240 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078390 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078247 :question/id #uuid "36c32777-8081-5431-a112-321738a3adab" :question/math-academy-id "e-3228"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "697f9bd8-9edf-5643-8cdb-a821b6dce09c" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049570} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050372}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079061} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079065} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275313} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275326} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275328} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275329} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275330} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332885} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332886} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332887} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396073} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396100} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432243} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432244} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432245} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432246}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Center and Radius of a Circle by Completing the Square"} :step/id #uuid "da3fbfb0-744f-5f91-919e-01f09b8883ea" :step/math-academy-id 10943 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079241}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079241 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078391 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078248 :question/id #uuid "b05d1090-fd7b-52eb-84a7-55377ead0dda" :question/math-academy-id "e-3229"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "98e3f3b4-8dfa-515c-90b1-09fb19d68144" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050044}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079067} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079203} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186238070} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186238071} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186238072} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275155} {:db/id #edb